<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philibert Tsiranana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philibert Tsiranana was the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, from 1959 to 1972. A teacher from the north-west, he founded the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> in 1956, became head of government of the autonomous Malagasy Republic in 1958 and led the island to independence on 26 June 1960, keeping close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Student and peasant protests in 1972 forced him to hand power to the army under Gabriel Ramanantsoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philibert-tsiranana.jpg" alt="Philibert Tsiranana" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: State of Israel Press Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philibert_Tsiranana_in_August_27th,_1961.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his official biography Tsiranana was born on 18 October 1912 at Ambarikorano in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, to Catholic cattle ranchers of the Tsimihety people. After his father died in 1923, his brother suggested that he attend a primary school, and he became a teacher. In 1946 he joined the Group of Student Communists and became a founding member of the Party of the Disinherited of Madagascar, PADESM. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the Malagasy Uprising of 1947 and so escaped its suppression. There he noticed that only 17 of 198 Malagasy students were coastal people, and he founded associations to close the cultural gap between the coastal peoples and those of the highlands, which the Merina resented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Tsiranana joined the French Socialist party in 1955 and was elected deputy for the western region to the French National Assembly in January 1956. On 28 December 1956 he founded the Social Democratic Party at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajanga">Majunga</a>, which benefited from the support of the colonial administration as it transferred executive power. He favoured strong autonomy rather than early independence, arguing that independence too early would bring economic dependence, and wished to remain close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A constitution modelled largely on that of France was adopted on 29 April 1959, with a president who was both head of state and head of government, and on 1 May Tsiranana was elected the first president of the Malagasy Republic, with 113 votes and one abstention.</p><p>To establish his image as father of independence he recalled the three deputies exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after 1947, Joseph Ravoahangy, Joseph Raseta and Jacques Rabemananjara, in July 1960, and brought two of them into his government. He declared that his country belonged to the Western world, and French advisers remained close to his government; he told the French living on the island that they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>'s nineteenth tribe. He also built links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. His own Malagasy Socialism, a pragmatic development policy, did not have a large economic effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1967 Tsiranana faced mounting criticism, and his health, troubled by cardiovascular disease, declined. Many Malagasy felt that political independence had not brought economic independence, since the French controlled the economy and most senior technical posts. In the presidential election of 30 January 1972 he ran without opposition and won 99.72 percent of the vote, but the opposition then took to the streets, and after farmer and student protests he granted full powers to Ramanantsoa, which ended the First Republic. After Richard Ratsimandrava was assassinated in 1975, Tsiranana was among 296 people charged. He was released for lack of evidence. He died on 16 April 1978 and is remembered as the Father of Independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philibert Tsiranana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Madagascar became the autonomous Malagasy Republic in October 1958 and fully independent on 26 June 1960 under President <b>Philibert Tsiranana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-madagascar">French Madagascar</a></li><li><blockquote>A constitution followed in 1959, and full independence came on 26 June 1960 under President <b>Philibert Tsiranana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/madagascar">Madagascar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ambarikorano, in what is now <a href="/countries/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-15.88,48.97&amp;year=1954" aria-label="Ambarikorano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Philibert Tsiranana"><image href="/maps/globes/g060s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4098 3848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4098 3848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-15.88,48.97&amp;year=1954">What was here in 1954? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
